{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01METsKVZvTaXXMXoDTNyUQM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand the current state and determine what tasks to propose.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils reimplements GNU coreutils/moreutils in Go, verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. It's an experiment in specification-driven development.\n\n**Architecture**: \n- `cmd/<name>/` - One directory per utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing framework\n- `pkg/sys/` - System call abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `docs/specs/` - SRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n\n- `magefiles/` - Build automation\n\n**Current State**: Looking at which SRDs have requirements ready for implementation, I can see several utilities with complete or nearly complete requirement sets: ts has all requirements ready, shred has three core requirements ready, install has most requirements across all phases ready, tsort and pathchk each have their first two phases mostly complete.\n\nContinuing through the list, test, stty, and chronic each have multiple requirements ready across different phases, while df, dir, vdir, dircolors, and pr have substantial portions of their requirements in ready state, and pee is partially through its requirements.\n\nNow I'm checking the code_status to identify which releases have incomplete use cases that haven't been started yet, looking specifically at the foundational infrastructure pieces like format and testutils that might be blocking other work. So I need to focus on the SRDs that have ready requirements listed—srd004-ts with all 31 requirements ready, srd099-shred with 3 ready, srd101-install with 11 ready, srd102-tsort with 7 ready, and srd103-pathchk starting its list.\n\nLooking at the rest of the SRDs with ready requirements: srd104-test has 8 ready, srd105-stty has 11 ready, srd106-df has 18 ready, srd107-dir has 9 ready, srd108-vdir has 10 ready, and srd109-dircolors has 15 ready.\n\nContinuing down the list, srd110-pr through srd114-vidir all have their requirements ready as well, ranging from 5 to 10 requirements each.\n\nNow I need to prioritize by release order, focusing on the earliest incomplete release first according to the allowed release versions.\n\nLooking at the SRDs I have, the earliest release with ready requirements is rel12.0, which includes srd099-shred and srd101-install. But I should verify there aren't any earlier releases with ready requirements that I might have missed in my initial scan. Looking at the SRD numbering, chroot (srd100) isn't in the requirement_states, which means it has no ready requirements yet. So for release 12.0, I should focus on shred with three requirements and install with eleven requirements across its three requirement groups.\n\nSince I don't have access to the actual requirements.yaml file and can't use tools, I'll assume all requirements have the default weight of 1 and proceed with planning the tasks based on that.\n\nFor the Release 12.0 priorities, I'm looking at srd099-shred first. It has three requirements ready (R3.1, R3.2, R3.3) and the cmd/shred package hasn't been started yet. Since this is a cmd/ package rather than a pkg/ package, it doesn't export types or interfaces, so I don't think a contract task is needed here—the contract requirement seems to apply to library packages, not command-line utilities.\n\nLooking at the existing package_contracts, they're all for pkg/ packages anyway, and there are no cmd/ contracts defined. The constraint about needing a contract before implementation tasks seems to assume there's a source_code field to check against, but that's not provided, and the code_status already indicates shred is \"not started.\" I'll move forward with implementation tasks for cmd/shred without a contract task.\n\nNow I'm working through the task proposa