e their exact specifications. I'm verifying the weight constraints for each task to ensure they stay within the four-point limit, then moving through the implementation and test tasks for both the shred and install commands. I'm noticing a contradiction here—the code status shows shred as \"not started,\" but the requirement states suggest R1.x and R2.x might already be complete, which would be inconsistent. Looking at the recent git commits, it seems the requirement states were just updated for this work, so they likely reflect the current progress more accurately than the stale code status. Since only R3.x are marked as ready, either R1.x and R2.x were recently completed or intentionally skipped, but either way I need to focus on what's actually ready to implement next.\n\nFor the YAML output, I should propose tasks for the ready R-items and let the stitch agent handle checking whether cmd/shred/main.go already exists and needs modification or creation. I also need to remember that contract tasks don't apply to cmd/ packages since they're package main with no exports, so I'll skip that step and go straight to proposing implementation tasks based on the requirement states.\n\nFollowing the canonical ordering constraint—documentation before code, contracts before implementations, types before consumers, implementation before tests, with alphabetical tie-breaking by file path—I'll order cmd/install before cmd/shred since \"install\" comes before \"shred\" alphabetically.\n\nSince shred is smaller and can be completed fully in one batch, I'll do shred implementation and tests together, then move to install's two implementation parts. I need to reconsider the dependencies since task 1 modifies the same file as task 0, so it must depend on task 0 completing first. Tasks 0 and 2 could run in parallel since they're independent packages, but the dependency system only supports single dependencies, so I'll set task 0 as a prerequisite for task 1 and leave task 2 independent