hred's, respecting the dependency chain. I need to include separate test tasks per P2, but with only 4 tasks available, I can't fit both implementation and tests for both utilities. Let me check whether P2 expects test tasks in this same batch or if deferring them is acceptable—it says to decompose features into up to three tasks in that order, but doesn't explicitly require all of them in one batch.\n\nGiven P3's constraint of max 4 tasks and the ability to create additional batches later, I'll go with Option A: focus on implementation tasks now and handle tests in the next round.\n\nSo my task breakdown is:\n- Task 0: cmd/install srd101-install R1.1-R1.4 (implementation) - no dependencies\n- Task 1: cmd/install srd101-install R2.1-R2.4 (implementation) - depends on Task 0\n- Task 2: cmd/install srd101-install R3.1-R3.3 (implementation) - depends on Task 1\n- Task 3: cmd/shred srd099-shred R3.1-R3.3 (implementation) - no dependencies\n\nNow I need to write detailed descriptions for each task. The SRD files follow the naming pattern like srd008-ls and srd004-ts, so I'm looking at srd101-install.yaml and srd099-shred.yaml in the docs/specs/software-requirements directory.\n\nFor the stitch agent to implement these tasks, I need to specify what it should read (the SRD, existing pkg/ code for file metadata), what files to create or modify (cmd/install/main.go and cmd/shred/main.go), and the specific requirements it needs to implement. I should also note the SIGPIPE handler and differential testing pattern that need to be included.\n\nNow I'm clarifying the mapping between task-local requirement IDs and the actual SRD requirement IDs—the task uses R1, R2, etc., but these map to SRD IDs like R1.1, R1.2, so I need to reference the SRD IDs directly in the requirements section to avoid confusion.\n\nWriting task description YAML... I'm sketching out the task structure with a weight of 4 points across the four requirements, and I need to specify the deliverable as code with clear references to the SRD sections, architecture documentation, and relevant system packages so the implementation agent has everything needed to build the install utility. I'm realizing the requirements are too vague without access to the actual SRD content. The stitch agent needs to read the SRD directly to find what R1.1 through R1.4 actually specify, so my requirements should reference those specific sections rather than trying to duplicate them. Since I can't read the SRD myself, the best approach is to write requirements that point to the SRD and let the agent pull the actual implementation details from there.\n\nFor the install command specifically, I know from the roadmap that it handles file copying with ownership, permissions, and directory creation—similar to GNU install with options like -d for directories, -o/-g for ownership, and -m for permissions. The SRD groups requirements into sections: R1.1-R1.4, R2.1-R2.4, and R3.1-R3.3, so I'll structure my requirements to match those groupings and keep the acceptance criteria focused on what's verifiable: compilation, successful builds, and correct behavior implementation.\n\nFor shred, the core functionality involves overwriting files with random data patterns across multiple passes, with optional truncation and unlinking. Since R3 appears to be the only ready group, I'm assuming R1 and R2 are already complete. I need to make sure each task references at most 4 SRD sub-requirements and targets the production code line range specified in the constraints, then format everything properly in YAML.\n\nLooking at the file count constraint—it says no more than 5-7 files, which is an upper bound, so creating just 1 file for the install task is acceptable. But I need to be careful about task dependencies. Since task 1 modifies cmd/install/main.go which was created in task 0, the description for task 1 needs to explicitly note that the file already exists and requires modification, so an agent executing it independently has all the context needed.\n\nLet me finalize the output now.\n\nOne more thing to verify—the constraint about contracts. Looking at the package_contracts section, it only lists contracts for pkg/ packages like srd001-testutils, srd002-sys, and others. Since cmd/install and cmd/shred are main packages that don't export anything, they don't need contracts. The contract requirement seems to apply only to pkg/ packages.\n\nNow I'm drafting the final YAML output. I need to be careful with the description field formatting since it uses a literal block scalar. Let me structure the tasks: Task 0 will handle the initial implementation of cmd/install covering requirements R1.1 through R1.4, Task 1 will continue with R2.1 through R2.4, and Task 2 will cover R3 and beyond.\n\nFor cmd/shred, since the earlier requirements aren't in ready state, the file likely already exists, so I'll use modify rather than create. I'm now writing out the complete YAML structure with proper formatting.\n\nLooking at the release dependencies, 12.0 only depends on 00.0, so there are no other blocking constraints. I've got exactly 4 tasks which meets the limit, and the line count distribution—roughly 100-200 lines per task across the install command—seems reasonable even if it's below the 300-line target, since this is a partial implementation of a utility.\n\nThe hard constraint is 4 sub-requirements per task maximum, which I'm respecting. Now I need to verify what GNU install actually does to make sure my task breakdown aligns with the real functionality—it handles file copying with optional ownership, permissions, directory creation, symbol stripping, and backup features. I have enough context now to write the task descriptions. Let me organize them in the canonical order: install before shred alphabetically, implementation before tests, and within install following the R1, R2, R3 sequence. So I'm setting up four tasks covering the install command's three requirement groups and the shred command's ready group, with appropriate dependencies between them.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":45159,"cache_read_input_tokens":11845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":45159},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ad31ef54-ffb0-4160-b8b1-6644e767d352","uuid":"57c943c6-14fa-45fe-887b-2e4b93c3d516"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01METsKVZvTaXXMXoDTNyUQM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils reimplements GNU coreutils/moreutils in Go, verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The project is an experiment in specification-driven code generation using the mage-claude-orchestrator pipeline.\n\n**Architecture**: Specifications in `docs/specs/` drive code generation into `cmd/<utility>/` packages, with shared infrastructure in `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), and `pkg/format/` (output formatting). Each utility has `main.go` + `<name>_test.go` with differential tests against GNU `g`-prefixed reference binaries.\n\n**Current state**: 33 releases defined (00.0 through 15.0). Many utilities are implemented. The `requirement_states` field shows which SRD requirements are still \"ready\" (not yet started). The earliest incomplete release with ready requirements is **12.0** (shred, install).\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\n**Release 12.0** is the earliest incomplete release with ready requirements:\n- `srd099-shred`: R3.1, R3.2, R3.3 (3 requirements ready -- R1/R2 groups presumably complete)\n- `srd101-install`: R1.1-R1.4, R2.1-R2.4, R3.1-R3.3 (11 requirements ready -- no code exists yet)\n- `srd100-chroot`: No requirements in ready state (not actionable)\n\nNo dependencies block 12.0 beyond rel00.0 which is already partially implemented.\n\n## Weight Budget Check\n\nAssuming default weight=1 for all requirements (weights not provided in context):\n\n- Task 0: install R1.1(1) + R1.2(1) + R1.3(1) + R1.4(1) = 4 -- OK\n- Task 1: install R2.1(1) + R2.2(1) + R2.3(1) + R2.4(1) = 4 -- OK\n- Task 2: install R3.1(1) + R3.2(1) + R3.3(1) = 3 -- OK\n- Task 3: shred R3.1(1) + R3.2(1) + R3.3(1) = 3 -- OK\n\nCanonical ordering: cmd/install (i) before cmd/shred (s) alphabetically; implementation tasks sequenced by requirement group.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/install srd101-install R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.4 (implementation)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd101-install.yaml (requirements R1.1 through R1.4)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (shared_protocols section for SIGPIPE and differential testing patterns)\n      - pkg/sys/sys.go (FileInfo, Stat, Lstat for file metadata)\n      - CLAUDE.md (build instructions and SIGPIPE handler requirement)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/install/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"install utility -- initial implementation covering srd101-install R1.1 through R1.4\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd101-install R1.1 as specified in the SRD. Read the SRD to determine the exact requirement.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd101-install R1.2 as specified in the SRD. Read the SRD to determine the exact requirement.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement srd101-install R1.3 as specified in the SRD. Read the SRD to determine the exact requirement.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement srd101-install R1.4 as specified in the SRD. Read the SRD to determine the exact requirement.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main() per the shared SIGPIPE protocol in ARCHITECTURE.yaml\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use go build -o bin/install ./cmd/install/ to verify compilation. The bin/ directory is git-ignored.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Use standard library os, io, and filepath packages for file operations. Import pkg/sys only if the SRD requires extended file metadata (Stat, Lstat, FileInfo).\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"This is the first of three implementation tasks for cmd/install. Implement only R1.1-R1.4. Later tasks will add R2.x and R3.x requirements.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/install/main.go exists and compiles with go vet ./cmd/install/...\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go build -o bin/install ./cmd/install/ succeeds without errors\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"All four SRD requirements R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, and R1.4 from srd101-install are implemented\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"The main function calls sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() before any other logic\"\n\n- index: 1\n  title: \"cmd/install srd101-install R2.1, R2.2, R2.3, R2.4 (implementation)\"\n  dependency: 0\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd101-install.yaml (requirements R2.1 through R2.4)\n      - cmd/install/main.go (existing implementation from prior task covering R1.1-R1.4)\n      - CLAUDE.md (build instructions)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/install/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: \"Add srd101-install R2.1 through R2.4 functionality to existing install implementation\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd101-install R2.1 as specified in the SRD. Read the SRD to determine the exact requirement.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd101-install R2.2 as specified in the SRD. Read the SRD to determine the exact requirement.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement srd101-install R2.3 as specified in the SRD. Read the SRD to determine the exact requirement.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement srd101-install R2.4 as specified in the SRD. Read the SRD to determine the exact requirement.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Extend the existing cmd/install/main.go from the prior task. Do not rewrite the file -- add the R2.x functionality to the existing flag parsing and execution logic.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use standard library os, io, and filepath packages for file operations. Import pkg/sys only if the SRD requires extended file metadata.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"This is the second of three implementation tasks. Implement only R2.1-R2.4. R1.x is already implemented. R3.x will be added in a subsequent task.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/install/main.go compiles with go vet ./cmd/install/...\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go build -o bin/install ./cmd/install/ succeeds without errors\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"All four SRD requirements R2.1, R2.2, R2.3, and R2.4 from srd101-install are implemented\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Previously implemented R1.x functionality is preserved and not broken\"\n\n- index: 2\n  title: \"cmd/install srd101-install R3.1, R3.2, R3.3 (implementation)\"\n  dependency: 1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd101-install.yaml (requirements R3.1 through R3.3)\n      - cmd/install/main.go (existing implementation from prior tasks covering R1.1-R1.4 and R2.1-R2.4)\n      - CLAUDE.md (build instructions)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/install/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: \"Add srd101-install R3.1 through R3.3 functionality to complete the install implementation\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd101-install R3.1 as specified in the SRD. Read the SRD to determine the exact requirement.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd101-install R3.2 as specified in the SRD. Read the SRD to determine the exact requirement.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement srd101-install R3.3 as specified in the SRD. Read the SRD to determine the exact requirement.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Extend the existing cmd/install/main.go from prior tasks. Do not rewrite the file -- add the R3.x functionality to the existing flag parsing and execution logic.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"This is the final implementation task for cmd/install.